latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Determine the influence type and border colors:\n   a. Check if the top and bottom rows are non-zero. If so, it's vertical influence.\n   b. Otherwise, it's horizontal influence (left and right columns are non-zero).\n   c. Store the border colors for each side (top/bottom or left/right).\n\n2. Create a deep copy of the input grid to modify.\n\n3. Get the dimensions of the grid (rows and columns).\n\n4. Calculate the midpoint:\n   a. For vertical influence: row_midpoint = rows // 2\n   b. For horizontal influence: col_midpoint = cols // 2\n\n5. Define the direction orders:\n   a. For vertical influence:\n      - Upper half: [(-1, 0), (-1, -1), (-1, 1)]\n      - Lower half: [(1, 0), (1, -1), (1, 1)]\n   b. For horizontal influence:\n      - Left half: [(0, -1), (-1, -1), (1, -1)]\n      - Right half: [(0, 1), (-1, 1), (1, 1)]\n\n6. Iterate through each cell in the grid:\n   a. If the cell is blue (value 1):\n      i. Determine which half it's in:\n         - For vertical: upper if row < row_midpoint, else lower\n         - For horizontal: left if col < col_midpoint, else right\n      ii. Set the color to spread:\n         - For vertical: top_color if in upper half, else bottom_color\n         - For horizontal: left_color if in left half, else right_color\n      iii. Get the appropriate direction order based on the half\n      iv. For each direction in the order:\n          - Calculate the new position\n          - If the new position is within the grid and the cell is black (0):\n            * Change the cell to the spreading color\n            * Break the loop (only change one cell per blue dot)\n\n7. Return the modified grid\n\nThis plan addresses the key insights from our reflection:\n- It correctly determines the influence type based on which opposite sides are non-zero.\n- It treats the grid in halves rather than quadrants, simplifying the logic.\n- The spreading always occurs towards the nearest colored border.\n- The order of checking cells is consistent and prioritizes the main direction of influence before checking diagonals.\n\nThis approach should work consistently for all the given examples and test cases, providing a more robust and generalized solution to the challenge."
